WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.181
Safe dynamic allocation also means handling memory pressure and resource limits.

2
00:00:04.181 --> 00:00:11.783
External size values from files, protocols, APIs, configuration, command-line arguments, and user input should be treated as untrusted until

3
00:00:11.783 --> 00:00:12.163
validated.

4
00:00:12.163 --> 00:00:17.864
A number that parses successfully may still be far outside the application's acceptable range.

5
00:00:17.864 --> 00:00:19.765
Apply reasonable maximums before allocation.

6
00:00:19.765 --> 00:00:28.127
The maximum should come from product requirements, service limits, device capacity, tenant policy, or data format rules, not just from the largest

7
00:00:28.127 --> 00:00:30.027
value a type can represent.

8
00:00:30.027 --> 00:00:35.729
A value can fit in a size type and still be unreasonable for the system.

9
00:00:35.729 --> 00:00:38.769
Define behavior for allocation failure and memory pressure.

10
00:00:38.769 --> 00:00:46.751
Depending on project policy, the program might return an error, throw, reject the request, degrade gracefully, shed work, or fail closed.

11
00:00:46.751 --> 00:00:52.833
The important point is that failure behavior is predictable and does not leave partial ownership behind.

12
00:00:52.833 --> 00:00:58.534
Large allocations can affect availability and stability even when they do not violate memory safety.

13
00:00:58.534 --> 00:01:03.475
They can increase latency, trigger paging, starve other work, or cause cascading failures.

14
00:01:03.475 --> 00:01:08.416
Defensive resource limits are part of secure engineering because they preserve predictable operation.

15
00:01:08.416 --> 00:01:11.457
Systems should fail safely when limits are exceeded.

16
00:01:11.457 --> 00:01:15.638
Error messages should be useful to operators without exposing sensitive internals.

17
00:01:15.638 --> 00:01:17.919
Cleanup should release any partial resources.

18
00:01:17.919 --> 00:01:24.000
Monitoring should make repeated allocation pressure visible so teams can tune limits and investigate abnormal workloads.
